[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This little mic right here is the Holland Lark A2. And it\'s one of the most affordable wireless microphones I have ever used. [music] And it\'s actually the followup to the LARK A1, which is a mic that I\'ve loved and used for the last year or so. But the LAR A2 has a brand new design and some new AI'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feature integration for better audio, battery life, and overall performance. So, today we\'re covering everything you need to know about the LAR A2. So, if you pick up this microphone, it\'s going to come with a nice cloth carrying case.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then inside of that carrying case is a charging case. That\'s a nice hard shell cover. Inside of that, you\'ll see your two wireless microphone transmitters. And then you have one USBC type audio receiver. And you\'ll get a'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'couple of windscreens as well as well as a charging cable. And all of that is for a price of $45. And they actually have some other kit variations that go as low as like $30 to get the LAR A2. Now, right away, as you compare the Lark A2'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the last version, the Lark A1, you\'ll notice some differences right off the gate. Now, the exterior case is actually a little bit smaller on the LAR A2, which I found interesting because the mic size, the actual body of the'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'microphone is larger on the LAR A2 than it is on the LAR A1. Now, the weight difference seems pretty minimal. The LAR A2 still is very lightweight, but you can definitely see that this is a larger'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'microphone in footprint. So, if you prefer a smaller mic design, the LAR A1 could still be a great option. Additionally, the LAR A2 features a more clipon design. So, there\'s a clip on the back of the microphone, and you clip'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that onto your collar or your shirt or even a hat. However you want to clip this on is totally fine, but it\'s designed to be more of a clip-on microphone versus the Lark A1 comes with just a magnet. So, you have to magnetize that to your shirt in order to clip it'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'onto yourself. Honestly, I like both designs. Sometimes I want to clip on a microphone and sometimes I want to magnetize it more like center chest. So I think there\'s a use case for either option here. It just kind of depends on your preference. Now when it comes to'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pairing the LAR A2 with your smartphone or computer devices, all you got to do is take the USBC receiver, plug that directly into your smartphone or computer or tablet and then pull out the mic or microphones that you want to use'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then they connect in just an instant. As soon as you take those out of the case, you\'re going to be paired in a matter of seconds and then you can start recording. Now, there are some settings that you can adjust with this microphone, and I\'ll get to those in just a minute. But the first thing I'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to do is do a series of sound tests and comparisons so you can get a baseline for how this microphone actually sounds. So, this first test is very much a baseline for how you would use this microphone in a typical situation. I\'m in an indoor environment.'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'My studio is not super sound treated, so there might be a little bit of echo going on, but it\'s a pretty typical use case for how you would use this microphone. You just pop it onto your shirt and start recording. Now, the second audio test would be unclipping'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this from your shirt and holding it in your hand like a social media style talking head video. We see this a lot of time with YouTube shorts or Instagram reels. Creators will grab a wireless microphone like this and hold it out in'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'front of them like a really tiny portable handheld microphone. And actually, the sound quality, I think, sounds a lot better because when you clip it onto your shirt, sometimes you get a little bit of that throat noise. So, when you bring it in front of your'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mouth, you\'re going to get some better and brighter sound quality. So, you could always try that with these microphones if you want to get just a little bit better sound out of these tiny little mics. Now, our third test is very similar. We\'ve actually just clipped the LAR A2 inside of a little'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wireless microphone handle and we threw on a windscreen. This is a little custom Think Media one that we\'ve made, but you can find super cheap versions of these out there for like $10 or $15. And this will greatly improve your overall sound'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality because again, it puts that microphone in a better position. And the windscreen is helping cut out some of the pllosives or just like bad sounds that happen when you\'re recording videos. These windscreens really help a lot. And for our fourth test, I just'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clipped it onto my hat right here. I see creators do this all the time. That\'s not something I really do, but if you want to know what this microphone would sound like if you clipped it onto your hat, it sounds like this. Now, for this next test, we\'re actually doing a'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comparison between the LAR A2 and then the last version, which was the LAR A1. So, now you\'re hearing the audio from the LAR A1. You can hear how that sounds. and then swapping back over to the Lark A2. Honestly, I think that these sound very similar in sound'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. You\'re not going to get a huge disparity between these two in terms of one sounding way better than the other. They\'re just kind of different designs, but this is how the sound quality sounds between the two mics. In terms of'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'battery life performance, a single transmitter has about 7 hours of battery life. And then with recharging from the case, you have about 42 hours total of run time between the two microphones if you\'re recharging in between takes.'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s also an AI power saving mode that you can turn on in the app. That gets you about a 25% efficiency gain, which can extend the run time to about 9 hours per microphone. There\'s also quick'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'charging available for these mics if you plug the mics directly into a USB cable. You can actually get one hour of shooting if you just plug that microphone in for only five minutes. And then same thing with the case, but the'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'case needs 10 minutes of charging to get you 1 hour of record time. Now, there are some physical controls on the microphone itself as well as the receiver. So, on the mic itself, if you click the yellow button on the side one time, you\'ll be able to turn on and off'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noise cancellation. When you turn it on, the light turns green, and then you turn it back off, the light will switch back to blue. Now, if you also click that button two times, it will mute the microphone. And the microphone light indicator is going to turn red. And then'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you would click it two more times to unmute the microphone. And then if you click that button three times, you can actually start and stop recordings on a smartphone. So you can use this as a camera control, which is really cool. If'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re recording on a smartphone and you\'re further away from your phone, but you still want to start and stop recording, you can do this with the LAR A2. On the receiver itself, it does have a USBC pass through port for charging. So your smartphone\'s not going to die'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even if you\'re spending a bunch of time recording with your smartphone. Additionally, there is a button on that receiver and you can click it one time to enable noise cancellation on the microphones. And then if you click it two times, it\'s actually going to mute'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the mics. Now, for a lot of people, just plugging the microphone in and relying on the default settings is what most people are going to do. But if you want maximum control out of this little microphone, you will want to download the Holly audio app, which is going to enable a lot more feature control out of'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this microphone. So, if you dive into the app here, you can actually control your noise cancellation levels from low, medium, and high. There\'s also a receiver volume control that you can slide up or down to change the receiver'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'levels if you need more or less gain to whatever device you\'re recording to. Additionally, there are some channel modes, so you can record in mono, stereo, or safe track, which is a really cool new feature out of the Lark A2. So,'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mono just merges both of your audio channels. It\'s going to output the same audio channel to the left and right channels. Stereo though is going to split the left and right channel. So if you\'re recording to both microphones, it'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will record one of them into the left channel, one of them into the right channel for independent audio control. And then Safe Track is going to record to the left and right channel at different volumes. So even if you\'re recording to both microphones at the same time, it will merge those into a'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mono signal, but then lower the volume of one of the channels so that your audio doesn\'t peak. And then if you\'re recording to only one microphone, it\'s going to just have that one mic signal recording at two different volume levels'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'again, so you don\'t ever peak. In the app, you also have some bellcanto presets, which are effectively just some EQ presets for your microphone. There\'s balance, which is how I\'m recording all of this video. I just kept it on balance'

[07:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the most standard EQ. Then there\'s also bright, which adds a highpass filter to make your audio sound just a little bit brighter and more crispy. And then there\'s the low, which is more of a lowass filter to make your audio sound'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'warmer and emphasize the deeper parts of your voice. You can also control the indicator light switch if you want those to toggle on or off. And there\'s even a mic identification button that you can enable, which then makes the indicator'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lights purple and yellow, so you know which is which. Otherwise, both indicator lights are going to be blue and you might lose track of which one you were using to record. And then in the top right corner, there are some other settings that you can enable like'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smart battery life to get the most battery performance out of your whole microphone system. There\'s also mic auto power off and then playback without unplugging. So you can play back audio on your smartphone without needing to'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unplug the unit itself. So by leveraging this app, you really do get access to a lot more features for the Lark A2. Now, I know this system is super affordable and meant to be very beginner friendly, but if I had to nitpick and try to come'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up with some things I would like to see improved out of maybe a Lark A3 in the future would be first, I think the design of the Lark A3 could be a great hybrid of what the Lark A1 and LAR A2'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'were. I actually liked the smaller size of the LAR A1 and they increased the size maybe 30 to 40% in the LAR A2. That\'s probably because it gets better range and a little bit better performance and AI features. All of'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those things are probably what is accounting for the increase in size. But if they could give us a little bit of a smaller microphone and bring back a magnet. So this microphone doesn\'t even ship with a magnet. So if you wanted to clip this on another location on your'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'body, you have to have a magnet that you\'ve had in the past and use that to clip it to your shirt. So I think combining aspects of the Lark A1 and A2 could make for a really great Lark A3. Additionally, it\'s probably a lot to ask'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for at this price point, but if we could get onboard recording, so you don\'t have to attach the Lark A2 to a smartphone or a computer in order to record, that would be really cool. Imagine just a'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'button right here. I could just click record and start recording audio for voiceovers or social media stuff. That would be really nice. But it is asking a lot for such a cheap microphone. So, who should purchase the Lark A2? Well, I think if you\'re a smartphone creator and'

[09:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want one of the most affordable microphones you can find, the LAR A2 is a great option. Regardless if you do YouTube videos or social media, or maybe you actually are somebody who works at your desk in a professional capacity, you could also use this microphone for'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'live streaming, coaching calls, video calls, or even working with Chat GBT and Claude and doing voice prompting or vibe coding. They actually do have some software and features available where you could customize operations for this'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'microphone to trigger specific tasks and operations inside of these AI platforms. So, there\'s lots of use cases for a simple microphone like this. And the best part of it all is this thing is so affordable at under $50. So, if you want'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to purchase the LAR A2, we\'ll put a link down in the description below so you can check it out. And if you want to check out some other Hollland microphones that are really incredible, you can click or tap this screen right here to watch that video. and I\'ll see you in the next'
